Well that's just fine and dandy. You can play that way, spawn a few,
kill them and move on. Good for you. You could even keep up a small-
scale farm (maybe a dozen puds) going indefinitely. However this
process is incredibly inefficient compared to the  traditional "fill a
hollowed level with P and then kill them all" approach. But let the
player think he's safe. Let him have his small scale farm and think
he's beaten the system. Trouble, is, just where is the line between
safe and unsafe? It won't be clearly defined, it will be a very vague
line, because it all relies on random dice rolls. Eventually he'll
overstep his safety limit for one reason or another (boredom, hoping
for quicker results), or he'll hit that one in a thousand (hundred/
million/ whatever you think is most balanced) combination of dice
rolls that triggers a chain reaction, or a couple of puddings will
sneak off out of sight and get up to mischief on their own without the
player's knowledge. As I said, the idea isn't to wipe out pudding
farming altogether, it's to turn it into a gamble: Introduce a chance
that it will all go horribly horribly wrong. The more you use it, the
higher the chance.

Again, we are grappling with an unavoidable truth of nethack that has
cropped up again and again recently: Pretty much anything but horribly
unbalanced unavoidable YAADs in nethack can be countered by spoiled,
cautious, optimal play, and it has been demonstrated that just about
any rolled character is ascendable. Despite this, most players (for
whatever reason- even many heavily spoiled ones) do not engage
consistently in non-optimal play and the vast majority of characters
do NOT ascend. The trick is to find a balance between "can be
consistently countered with optimal play" and "insufficient
opportunity to counter makes it a random dice roll of death."

There are several apporaches to this problem: One of them- the one I
like- is to appeal to the player's impatience or greed to tempt them
into non-optimal play:
"This floating eye only has 2 HP left, if I swipe it in melee I'll
almost certainly kill it before it can freeze me"
"I should dump this loot and unburden myself before those soldier ant
gets here, but I'm almost at the altar...
"Oh, I know I ought to wait until I find a safer method of diluting
this potion, but I want it now/ can't be bothered: I'll just dip it in
this fountain. I'll probably get away with it." "
And the player usually does get away with it- after all, if they never
did, they wouldn't be tempted to try. That's what make corner-cases
and low-probability deaths so appealing, and worthy of consideration.
Lots of small possibilities will add up to a very high possibiity, and
It's when that small chance of something really bad happening actually
happens that the game gets interesting, and the player is in real
danger of YASD.

*That's* the elegance of nethack, not blind adhesion to consistency
and undefined "mainstay rules".

Automating optimal play is difficult, but it has been demonstrated for
pud farming. The added complication of culling unfavourable mutations
in farmed puddings before they get a chance to spread their abilities
would defeat the current bot, and make the creation of a replacement
bot harder.


